<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zambezi</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Zambezi is the fourth-longest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, flowing from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, along the border of Zambia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>, and across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>. It was a major route for trade between the interior and the coast. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutapa-empire">Mutapa Empire</a> arose on the plateau south of the Zambezi, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rozvi-empire">Rozvi Empire</a> channelled gold exports through trading fairs such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zumbo">Zumbo</a> on the river.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zambezi.jpg" alt="Zambezi" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Brian McMorrow · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zambezi_River_at_junction_of_Namibia,_Zambia,_Zimbabwe_%26_Botswana.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Barotseland grew out of the Bulozi floodplain of the upper <b>Zambezi</b> in what is now western Zambia, a broad grassland flooded each year between about January and June.</blockquote><a href="/empire/barotseland">Barotseland · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Zimbabwe grew up on the granite country of the Zimbabwe plateau, between the Limpopo and <b>Zambezi</b> rivers, where Bantu-speaking farmers had lived since the first millennium.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-zimbabwe">Kingdom of Zimbabwe · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mutapa Empire, also known as the state of the Mwene Mutapa, arose in the mid-fifteenth century in the region between the <b>Zambezi</b> and Limpopo rivers in present-day Zimbabwe and Mozambique.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mutapa-empire">Mutapa Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>He then overran the older Torwa state of Butua in the south-west and, in campaigns of 1693–1695, expelled Portuguese traders, missionaries and prazo-holders (prazeros) from the plateau interior, driving them back towards the <b>Zambezi</b> valley and the Mozambican lowlands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rozvi-empire">Rozvi Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Ovimbundu traders organised long-distance caravans reaching the Lunda lands and the upper <b>Zambezi</b>, dealing in slaves, then ivory, beeswax and, in the late 19th century, wild rubber.</blockquote><a href="/state/bailundo-kingdom">Bailundo Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>North of the <b>Zambezi</b> it governed North-Western Rhodesia and North-Eastern Rhodesia, later Northern Rhodesia.</blockquote><a href="/state/company-rule-in-rhodesia">Company rule in Rhodesia</a></li></ul></section>
